  "full_response": "Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) and User Datagram Protocol (UDP) are two fundamental transport layer protocols in the Internet Protocol Suite (TCP/IP). They serve different purposes and have distinct characteristics. Here’s a detailed comparison:\n\n### **1. Connection Orientation**\n- **TCP**:\n  - **Connection-oriented**: Establishes a connection (via a three-way handshake) before data transfer and ensures a reliable, ordered delivery of data.\n  - Uses acknowledgments (ACKs) to confirm receipt of data.\n  - Suitable for applications where reliability is critical (e.g., web browsing, email, file transfers).\n\n- **UDP**:\n  - **Connectionless**: Sends data without establishing a connection, making it faster but less reliable.\n  - No handshake or acknowledgment mechanism.\n  - Suitable for real-time applications where speed is more important than reliability (e.g., video streaming, online gaming, VoIP).\n\n### **2. Reliability**\n- **TCP**:\n  - **Reliable**: Guarantees that data arrives intact and in order.\n  - Uses sequence numbers, checksums, and retransmission of lost packets.\n  - Flow control and congestion control mechanisms prevent overwhelming the receiver.\n\n- **UDP**:\n  - **Unreliable**: Does not guarantee delivery, ordering, or error checking.\n  - No retransmission of lost packets; applications must handle errors if needed.\n  - Faster due to minimal overhead.\n\n### **3. Speed & Overhead**\n- **TCP**:\n  - **Slower**: Higher overhead due to connection setup, acknowledgments, and error recovery.\n  - More suitable for applications where data integrity is prioritized over speed.\n\n- **UDP**:\n  - **Faster**: Lower overhead since it skips connection setup and error checking.\n  - Ideal for real-time applications where minor data loss is acceptable (e.g., live video, DNS lookups).\n\n### **4. Data Ordering**\n- **TCP**:\n  - **Ordered delivery**: Uses sequence numbers to ensure packets arrive in the correct order.\n  - Reorders packets if they arrive out of sequence.\n\n- **UDP**:\n  - **No ordering**: Packets may arrive out of order, or not at all.\n  - Applications must handle ordering if needed.\n\n### **5. Error Checking & Recovery**\n- **TCP**:\n  - **Error recovery**: Detects errors via checksums and retransmits lost or corrupted packets.\n  - Ensures data integrity.\n\n- **UDP",
